<section class="wrap">
  <header class="essay-header">
    <h1>Desktop app</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The pipeline that drew every map on this site, behind a window, for
      people who do not use a terminal. It runs on macOS and Windows. It
      is new: expect rough edges, and expect things to change between
      releases.
    </p>
  </header>
  <p>
    Give it a city&rsquo;s published GTFS timetable and it makes the same
    things the <a href="{{ '/atlas/' | url }}">atlas</a> is made of: a
    schematic map straightened onto a 45&deg; grid, and a running animation of
    the service day across it. Then it exports them as stills, reels and GIFs
    sized for Instagram, LinkedIn, Bluesky and X. Everything happens
    on your own computer.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Start from a sample city</strong>, one of the networks the atlas
    already draws: one press makes a project, downloads the feed and lays it
    out. Or <strong>start from your own feed</strong>, a GTFS zip on your
    computer or one at a web address.
  </p>
  <p>
    A project opens as a notebook of six numbered cells, under a map that
    stays in view while you work down them:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Data.</strong> Read what is in the feed: its routes,
      operators and service window, and the network drawn where it really
      runs. Choose which mode and which operator to keep.</li>
    <li><strong>Process.</strong> Lay the network out, stage by stage, and
      read what the build had to fudge. The layout is computed once and
      stored, so every map and export drawn from it afterwards matches.</li>
    <li><strong>Frame and service day.</strong> Pick the day the map is drawn
      for, scrub its clock, play the day and set the speed.</li>
    <li><strong>Style.</strong> A dark or a sepia map.</li>
    <li><strong>Lines.</strong> Each line&rsquo;s colour, and the order the
      lines stack in.</li>
    <li><strong>Export.</strong> A still, a reel or a GIF, from presets for
      each platform.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

